+<details>
+<summary>Pre-roll snapshot starvation — root-caused and fixed architecturally — ✅ DONE 2026-07-10 (follow-up to the closeout above)</summary>
+
+Diagnostic logging (`FLICKER-DIAG`-tagged, added this session) found `resolveRetainedBlend`'s retention fallback — shipped in the closeout entry above as a rare safety net — was actually activating on **effectively every single video-video boundary**, not the rare case it was designed for. The retention fix closed the symptom (hard cut) but never re-examined the budget: `PRE_ROLL_LEAD_S` (0.8s) was sized against a **"~200ms per video" estimate that a controlled in-app measurement disproved for real content** — real `<video>` `seekVideo` cost (timed via `awaitSeeked`) is **747.6ms uncontended and 998.2ms when `useWebCodecsPreview`'s own decode-ahead sessions (current + next) are concurrently active** (continuous during ordinary playback, not just near boundaries). Against that real ~1000ms worst case, 800ms had negative margin — a deterministic miss, not an occasional near-miss.
+
+**Fix — four parts, architectural, not a timeout bump:**
+- **Real-margin budget:** `PRE_ROLL_LEAD_S` → `BASE_PRE_ROLL_LEAD_S`, 0.8s → 1.8s (~1.8x margin over the measured 998.2ms contended worst case).
+- **Adaptive lead + bounded retry:** new pure `computeAdaptiveLeadS` widens the NEXT boundary's lead using the previous pre-roll render's own measured latency (1.5x safety factor, 3.5s cap); new pure `renderPreRollWithRetry` retries a rejected render exactly once — previously a genuinely *failed* render (real seek error, or `awaitSeeked`'s 5s hard timeout) had zero recovery path and left that boundary permanently dependent on retention for the rest of the session.
+- **Contention reduction at the source:** new `VideoDecoderPool.setPreRollPriorityIds`/`shouldDeferDecodeAhead` (`videoDecoderPool.ts`) — a third pool set, independent of the two eviction-protection sets, that gates *starting new decode-ahead work* rather than eviction survival. `useTransitionPreview.ts` declares the active transition pair as priority for the life of the pre-roll/active window; `useWebCodecsPreview.ts` skips starting brand-new decode-ahead for a "next" segment outside that pair, freeing decoder bandwidth for the transition-critical seeks. Bounded, accepted trade-off: that off-transition segment's own decode-ahead warm-up is skipped for that one boundary crossing (no worse than an ordinary cold start), not retried mid-window.
+- **Retention preserved unchanged** as the safety net for whatever residual failures the above don't prevent — expected to now activate rarely instead of on every boundary.
+- **Export confirmed unaffected** — same reasoning as the closeout entry above (direct synchronous per-frame render, no live-session tracking); not touched.
+- Tests: `useTransitionPreview.test.ts` +20 cases (`computeAdaptiveLeadS`, `renderPreRollWithRetry` — including realistic-timing regressions pinned to the measured 747.6ms/998.2ms values via `performance.now()` spies, replacing the old disproven 200-400ms assumption — and a budget-headroom-vs-retention-rarity check), `videoDecoderPool.test.ts` +9 cases (priority/deferral contract, incl. a simulated-2-concurrent-decode-session scenario). `tsc --noEmit` clean, `vitest` **437/437** (up from 415). Files touched: `src/hooks/useTransitionPreview.ts` (+test), `src/hooks/useWebCodecsPreview.ts`, `src/services/videoDecoderPool.ts` (+test). Docs: `docs/webgl-architecture-plan.md` updated with the measured numbers (Progress Tracker + Section 1.2), replacing the disproven "~200ms per video" estimate throughout.
+</details>
